        ":question/id": "fa483285-7fb1-55f0-9e07-a601fc5a4d37",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-237946",
        ":question/problem": "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{3x}-9\\cdot {5}^{3x}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{3x}-9\\cdot {5}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-9)\\cdot {5}^{3x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-9=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{3x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-9=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x-3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,3.$\n\n- The equation ${5}^{3x}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{3x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-3\\,\\text{and}\\,x=3.\n$$"
      },
