        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-264772",
        ":question/problem": "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x}+4x\\cdot {3}^{x}-48\\cdot {3}^{x}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x}+4x\\cdot {3}^{x}-48\\cdot {3}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+4x-48){3}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+4x-48=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{3}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}+4x-48=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}+2x-24) & =0 \\\\ 2(x+6)(x-4) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-6,4.$\n\n- The equation $2=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${3}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${3}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-6\\,\\text{and}\\,x=4.\n$$"
      },
