        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-264778",
        ":question/problem": "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x+3}-7x\\cdot {2}^{x+3}+3\\cdot {2}^{x+3}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x+3}-7x\\cdot {2}^{x+3}+3\\cdot {2}^{x+3} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-7x+3){2}^{x+3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-7x+3=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{2}^{x+3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-7x+3=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x-1)(x-3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{2},3.$\n\n- The equation ${2}^{x+3}=0$ has no solutions because ${2}^{x+3}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=3.\n$$"
      },
