        ":question/problem": "What is the product of solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x+3}-9x\\cdot {6}^{x+3}+4\\cdot {6}^{x+3}=0?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x+3}-9x\\cdot {6}^{x+3}+4\\cdot {6}^{x+3} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-9x+4){6}^{x+3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-9x+4=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{6}^{x+3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-9x+4=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x-1)(x-4)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{2},4.$\n\n- The equation ${6}^{x+3}=0$ has no solutions because ${6}^{x+3}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{2},4,$ and their product is $\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4=2.$"
