        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-88454",
        ":question/problem": "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{3x}-25\\cdot {4}^{3x}=0?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{3x}-25\\cdot {4}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-25)\\cdot {4}^{3x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-25=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{4}^{3x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-25=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+5)(x-5)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-5,5.$\n\n- The equation ${4}^{3x}=0$ has no solutions because ${4}^{3x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-5,5,$ and their product is $(-5)\\cdot 5=-25.$"
      },
