        ":question/id": "d3d7afbe-c052-55bc-910e-6663c48abfcd",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-88459",
        ":question/problem": "What is the product of solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+12x\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+10\\cdot {2}^{x-1}=0?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+12x\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+10\\cdot {2}^{x-1} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+12x+10){2}^{x-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+12x+10=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{2}^{x-1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}+12x+10=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n2(x+1)(x+5)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-5,-1.$\n\n- The equation ${2}^{x-1}=0$ has no solutions because ${2}^{x-1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-5,-1,$ and their product is $(-5)\\cdot (-1)=5.$"
      }
    ],
    ":knowledge-point/title": "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"
